# 13. PAGE TRANSITION ARCHITECTURE

Use a maintainable shared route-transition system.

Suggested conceptual structure:

```text
App
 └── CustomerNavigationShell
      ├── MainPageTransitionViewport
      │    ├── HomePage
      │    ├── OrderHistoryPage
      │    └── ProfilePageShell
      │         ├── ProfileScrollableContent
      │         └── FixedProfileHomePill
      └── NavigationTransitionController
```

Adapt to the actual router and layout architecture.

### Technical guidance

- Use the existing router.
- Use Framer Motion/Motion if already installed and appropriate.
- Otherwise use efficient CSS transforms and a well-defined transition state machine.
- Keep both outgoing and incoming page surfaces mounted for the transition duration.
- Render them within a clipped viewport-width transition stage.
- Keep navigation layering coordinated.
- Restore normal event handling and scrolling after completion.
- Prevent stale transitions from overriding new route state.

Do not implement this as a fragile collection of arbitrary timeout callbacks.

### Visual positioning

The outgoing and incoming pages must be properly aligned.

For an entry from the right, use viewport-based horizontal positions, not guessed pixel offsets.

Example concept:

```text
HOME → PROFILE

Initial:
[ HOME ]
          [ PROFILE outside right ]

During:
[ HOME moving left ][ PROFILE moving left ]

Final:
[ PROFILE ]
```

And reverse for returning Home.

These diagrams explain motion direction; follow the actual reference video for visual refinement.

